.TH SET_ITERATOR_ERASE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_iterator_erase \- Erase the current element from the set using an iterator

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_iterator_erase(set_iterator_t* it);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_iterator_erase\fR function erases the current element from the set using an iterator. It takes one argument: the iterator. The function returns 1 if the element is successfully erased, and 0 if the iterator is invalid or the element is not found.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB it
The iterator to use to erase the current element.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the element is successfully erased, and 0 if the iterator is invalid or the element is not found.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_iterator_erase\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    set_iterator_t it;
    set_init(&set);

    set_inserts(&set, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set, "cherry");

    set_foreach(&set, it, x, n) {
        if(strcmp(x, "banana") == 0) {
            set_iterator_erase(&it);
        }
    }

    printf("Set contains %d elements after erasing 'banana':\n", set_size(&set));
    set_foreach(&set, it, x, n) {
        printf("%.*s\n", (int)n, x);
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef